  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness — specifically, the extent to which the probability mass is concentrated on the unfavorable (zero) outcome. The individual does not mention the zero outcome probabilities or compare the distribution of probabilities between the two lotteries in terms of skewness. Instead, they focus on the prize size and the winning probability, concluding that the 0.2% chance in B is irrelevant due to the larger prize in A. This reasoning is inconsistent with the decision reason, which would prefer the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., the one with a higher probability of zero outcome). Lottery A has a 99.9% chance of 0, which is higher than B's 99.8%, so A is more right-skewed. However, the individual does not recognize or use this feature. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual's justification is based on prize size and winning probability, not on skewness. There is no reference to the unfavorable outcome probabilities or the structure of the distribution. Hence, the reason was not used.",
